urrent":"INCENP","gene_id":"ENSG00000149503","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":true,"nucleus_direction":"lower in AD","nucleus_padj":9.52024e-42,"nucleus_log2fc":-0.12355,"nucleus_score":-13.6014,"pct_ad":0.245448,"pct_control":0.27073,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.742191,"donor_log2fc":-0.0701492,"donor_lfcse":0.0674649,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":-0.0126363},{"cell_type":"In","gene_symbol":"INCENP","gene_symbol_current":"INCENP","gene_id":"ENSG00000149503","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":true,"nucleus_direction":"lower in AD","nucleus_padj":3.70921e-05,"nucleus_log2fc":-0.0704388,"nucleus_score":-4.21092,"pct_ad":0.0991882,"pct_control":0.105457,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.879537,"donor_log2fc":-0.0433453,"donor_lfcse":0.0767043,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":-0.00322641},{"cell_type":"Mic","gene_symbol":"INCENP","gene_symbol_current":"INCENP","gene_id":"ENSG00000149503","nucleus_tested":false,"nucleus_significant":null,"nucleus_direction":"not tested","nucleus_padj":null,"nucleus_log2fc":null,"nucleus_score":null,"pct_ad":null,"pct_control":null,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.964948,"donor_log2fc":0.0355946,"donor_lfcse":0.14357,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.00336366},{"cell_type":"OPC","gene_symbol":"INCENP","gene_symbol_current":"INCENP","gene_id":"ENSG00000149503","nucleus_tested":false,"nucleus_significant":null,"nucleus_direction":"not tested","nucleus_padj":null,"nucleus_log2fc":null,"nucleus_score":null,"pct_ad":null,"pct_control":null,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.999342,"donor_log2fc":-0.223818,"donor_lfcse":0.188537,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":-6.62465e-06},{"cell_type":"Oli","gene_symbol":"INCENP","gene_symbol_current":"INCENP","gene_id":"ENSG00000149503","nucleus_tested":false,"nucleus_significant":null,"nucleus_direction":"not tested","nucleus_padj":null,"nucleus_log2fc":null,"nucleus_score":null,"pct_ad":null,"pct_control":null,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.968306,"donor_log2fc":-0.0407268,"donor_lfcse":0.131488,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":-0.00245766}],"modules":[],"degas_bulk_de":false,"drug_target":false}},"description":[{"gene_id":"ENSG00000149503","entrez_id":["3619"],"uniprot":["Q9NQS7"],"ncbi_summary":"In mammalian cells, 2 broad groups of centromere-interacting proteins have been described: constitutively binding centromere proteins and 'passenger,' or transiently interacting, proteins (reviewed by Choo, 1997). The constitutive proteins include CENPA (centromere protein A; MIM 117139), CENPB (MIM 117140), CENPC1 (MIM 117141), and CENPD (MIM 117142). The term 'passenger proteins' encompasses a broad collection of proteins that localize to the centromere during specific stages of the cell cycle (Earnshaw and Mackay, 1994 [PubMed 8088460]). These include CENPE (MIM 117143); MCAK (MIM 604538); KID (MIM 603213); cytoplasmic dynein (e.g., MIM 600112); CliPs (e.g., MIM 179838); and CENPF/mitosin (MIM 600236). The inner centromere proteins (INCENPs) (Earnshaw and Cooke, 1991 [PubMed 1860899]), the initial members of the passenger protein group, display a broad localization along chromosomes in the early stages of mitosis but gradually become concentrated at centromeres as the cell cycle progresses into mid-metaphase. During telophase, the proteins are located within the midbody in the intercellular bridge, where they are discarded after cytokinesis (Cutts et al., 1999 [PubMed 10369859]).[supplied by OMIM, Mar 2008].","ncbi_source":"NCBI Gene summary (RefSeq), via MyGene.info","uniprot_function":[{"accession":"Q9NQS7","text":"Component of the chromosomal passenger complex (CPC), a complex that acts as a key regulator of mitosis. The CPC complex has essential functions at the centromere in ensuring correct chromosome alignment and segregation and is required for chromatin-induced microtubule stabilization and spindle assembly. Acts as a scaffold regulating CPC localization and activity. The C-terminus associates with AURKB or AURKC, the N-terminus associated with BIRC5/survivin and CDCA8/borealin tethers the CPC to the inner centromere, and the microtubule binding activity within the central SAH domain directs AURKB/C toward substrates near microtubules (PubMed:12925766, PubMed:15316025, PubMed:27332895). The flexibility of the SAH domain is proposed to allow AURKB/C to follow substrates on dynamic microtubules while ensuring CPC docking to static chromatin (By similarity). Activates AURKB and AURKC (PubMed:27332895). Required for localization of CBX5 to mitotic centromeres (PubMed:21346195). Controls the kinetochore localization of BUB1 (PubMed:16760428)."}],"uniprot_source":"UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot Function, release 2026_04","retrieved":"2026-10-21"}],"structures":[],"alphafold_accessions":[]}